const destinationProfiles = {
  ladakh: { overview:'Ladakh is a high-altitude Himalayan journey of wide-open valleys, cobalt lakes, prayer flags and roads that feel like an adventure in themselves. A well-designed first trip balances Leh acclimatisation with Nubra Valley, Pangong Lake, monasteries and high passes so that every day feels rewarding without pushing the body too quickly.', planningNotes:'Altitude is the most important part of planning. Keep your first 24 to 36 hours light, drink water regularly, avoid strenuous activity on arrival and follow your trip captain’s acclimatisation advice. Roads are weather-led, so keep buffer time and carry warm layers even in peak summer.', idealDuration:'6 to 8 days', gettingThere:'Leh is reached by flight from major Indian cities. Road journeys via Srinagar or Manali are seasonal and usually best planned from late spring through early autumn, subject to local road updates.', localInsight:'Spend an unhurried evening around Leh market, try local Ladakhi food and visit a monastery early in the day. These quiet moments add as much character as Pangong Lake or Khardung La.' },
  spiti: { overview:'Spiti Valley is a dramatic cold-desert road trip where high mountains, old monasteries, tiny villages and star-filled nights set the rhythm. The classic circuit through Shimla, Kinnaur, Kaza, Key and Chandratal is ideal for travellers who enjoy long scenic drives and landscapes that change every few hours.', planningNotes:'This is a high-altitude route, so a gradual ascent is the safer and more comfortable way to travel. Mobile signal, fuel stations and medical services can be limited in remote stretches; keep cash, warm layers, a reusable bottle and a flexible approach to road closures.', idealDuration:'7 to 9 days', gettingThere:'Most first-time trips begin from Delhi and enter through Shimla and Kinnaur, returning via Manali when the Kunzum route is open. Shared cabs, private vehicles and curated group departures are the most practical ways to cover the circuit.', localInsight:'Wake up early in Kaza or a nearby village, speak with local hosts and slow down at a monastery. Spiti becomes memorable when you allow the landscape and village life to set the pace.' },
  manali: { overview:'Manali is an easy Himalayan escape that combines river valleys, snow views, adventure activities and a lively cafe culture. It can be a quick weekend break, a family holiday or the gateway to longer journeys toward Lahaul, Spiti and Ladakh.', planningNotes:'Travel time from Delhi or Chandigarh can be long on busy weekends, so overnight transfers or an extra night help keep the holiday relaxed. Snow conditions vary by month; pack waterproof shoes and warm layers for Solang or higher routes, while summer visitors should still carry a light jacket.', idealDuration:'3 to 5 days', gettingThere:'Manali is commonly reached by overnight Volvo, private car or cab from Delhi and Chandigarh. Bhuntar Airport is the nearest airport, followed by a mountain road transfer.', localInsight:'Old Manali is best explored on foot in the morning or early evening. Pair one active Solang day with a slower village, riverside or cafe day for a much better balance.' },
  kerala: { overview:'Kerala brings together tea-covered hills, backwaters, beaches, spice gardens and a deeply rooted food culture. Munnar, Thekkady, Alleppey, Kochi and Varkala can be combined into a relaxed route that feels varied without requiring difficult travel days.', planningNotes:'The state has distinct microclimates: hill stations can be cool and wet while the coast stays warm. Build in adequate road time between Munnar, Thekkady and Alleppey, carry light rain protection, and reserve houseboats or popular boutique stays early in peak season.', idealDuration:'5 to 7 days', gettingThere:'Kochi, Thiruvananthapuram and Kozhikode are the main air gateways. Private road transfers are the most comfortable way to connect hill country, backwaters and the coast.', localInsight:'Choose one experience that slows the route down—a spice walk, a local cooking session, a sunset canoe ride or an early tea-estate walk—rather than treating Kerala only as a checklist.' },
  meghalaya: { overview:'Meghalaya is a lush northeast journey of misty plateaus, limestone caves, clear rivers, waterfalls and living root bridges. Shillong, Cherrapunji, Dawki and Mawlynnong form a strong first route for travellers who want natural beauty, local culture and soft adventure.', planningNotes:'Weather changes quickly and monsoon rain can be intense, so waterproof footwear and a rain layer are useful almost year-round. Road distances may look short but hill driving is slow; plan one major experience per day and check local guidance before cave, river or trekking activities.', idealDuration:'5 to 7 days', gettingThere:'Guwahati is the primary gateway, with onward road transfers to Shillong and the Khasi and Jaintia Hills. Private cabs or organised group transfers are the most convenient option.', localInsight:'The Umngot River is most magical in softer morning light, while root-bridge walks are more enjoyable with a slower pace, local guide and time to understand the communities that protect them.' },
  bali: { overview:'Bali is a colourful island journey of rice terraces, temple rituals, surf-lined shores, dramatic cliffs and creative neighbourhoods. A balanced first itinerary pairs Ubud’s cultural heart with Nusa Penida, Uluwatu or Seminyak, leaving room for pool time and unplanned evenings.', planningNotes:'Bali is best enjoyed without changing hotels every night. Base yourself in one or two areas, allow for traffic between regions, dress respectfully at temples and carry reef-safe sun protection for beach and boat days. Check passport, visa and travel-insurance requirements before flying.', idealDuration:'6 to 8 days', gettingThere:'Ngurah Rai International Airport in Denpasar is Bali’s main arrival point. Private airport transfers and local drivers are convenient for day trips across the island.', localInsight:'A small local warung meal, sunrise around the rice terraces and a ceremony or temple visit with respectful dress offer a richer experience than rushing only between beach clubs and photo spots.' },
  thailand: { overview:'Thailand is an energetic mix of street food, ornate temples, tropical beaches, island hopping and easy-going social evenings. Bangkok, Krabi, Phuket and nearby islands work well together for a first trip that blends city culture with coast time.', planningNotes:'Plan flights and island transfers with breathing room, especially in wet-season months. Keep lightweight clothing, sun protection and modest temple-covering layers ready. Visa, passport validity and entry conditions should be verified before departure.', idealDuration:'5 to 7 days', gettingThere:'Bangkok is the main international gateway, with domestic flights and road transfers connecting Phuket, Krabi and other beach regions. Airport-to-hotel transfers are easily arranged in advance.', localInsight:'Make time for an evening food market and a quieter long-tail boat outing. Thailand feels more memorable when one part of the itinerary is deliberately slower than the nightlife and sightseeing schedule.' },
  nepal: { overview:'Nepal combines Himalayan panoramas, ancient temple towns, lakeside Pokhara and welcoming mountain culture. Kathmandu, Bhaktapur, Nagarkot and Pokhara make an accessible first journey, while trekkers can extend the route into Annapurna or Everest-region trails with the right preparation.', planningNotes:'Check passport, visa and travel-insurance requirements before booking. Mountain weather changes quickly, so itinerary buffers and layered clothing matter even outside winter. For higher treks, choose experienced local support and allow enough time for acclimatisation.', idealDuration:'5 to 8 days', gettingThere:'Kathmandu’s Tribhuvan International Airport is the primary gateway. Overland entry is also possible through selected India–Nepal border points, followed by private road transfers or domestic flights.', localInsight:'Beyond the postcard mountain views, walk the heritage lanes of Bhaktapur, try a local Newari meal and give Pokhara an extra sunset evening by the lake.' },
  'spiti-winter': { overview:'A Winter Spiti trip is for travellers who want the quiet, snow-covered side of the Himalayas. The route is weather-led, so the experience is planned around acclimatisation, reliable stays and enough buffer for changing road conditions.', planningNotes:'Winter travel works best with a flexible mindset. Keep an extra day in hand, choose warm layered clothing and follow local road guidance rather than treating the itinerary as fixed.', idealDuration:'6 to 8 days', gettingThere:'Usually approached from Shimla through the lower Kinnaur route, subject to local road and weather conditions.', localInsight:'Winter villages are calm and services can be limited; travelling with a local support team makes planning far easier.' },
  kashmir: { overview:'Kashmir brings together alpine meadows, lake life, Mughal-era gardens and mountain villages in one memorable Himalayan holiday. A well-paced route leaves room for Srinagar, Gulmarg, Pahalgam and the slower moments in between.', planningNotes:'Pack layers for cool evenings even in summer, keep photo ID handy for hotel check-in, and leave room in the schedule for weather-related changes in higher areas.', idealDuration:'5 to 7 days', gettingThere:'Srinagar is the usual gateway, with onward road transfers to Gulmarg, Pahalgam and nearby valleys.', localInsight:'A shikara ride, a walk through old Srinagar and a meal featuring Kashmiri flavours make the journey feel more rooted than a quick sightseeing loop.' },
  uttarakhand: { overview:'Uttarakhand is made for mountain road trips, riverside breaks and temple-town culture. From Mussoorie and Nainital to Rishikesh, Auli and quieter hill villages, it can be shaped into a relaxed family holiday or an active escape.', planningNotes:'Hill drives take longer than maps suggest. Start early, carry light rain protection in the monsoon months, and keep your plan flexible around road and weather updates.', idealDuration:'4 to 8 days', gettingThere:'Delhi is a common starting point, with rail and road connections through Dehradun, Haridwar, Kathgodam and Rishikesh.', localInsight:'The best routes combine one iconic stop with a smaller village or forest stay, rather than trying to cover the whole state in a few days.' },
  himachal: { overview:'Himachal Pradesh offers an easy mix of snow peaks, cedar forests, café towns and road-trip views. Manali, Shimla, Dharamshala, Kasol and Jibhi each have a distinct pace, making the state ideal for repeat travel.', planningNotes:'Choose a compact circuit instead of overloading the route. Mountain weather changes quickly, so warm layers and a weather-aware driving plan matter across seasons.', idealDuration:'4 to 7 days', gettingThere:'Most trips begin from Delhi or Chandigarh, with road connections into the Kullu, Shimla and Kangra regions.', localInsight:'Early mornings are best for scenic drives; evenings are better reserved for local cafés, village walks or simply slowing down.' },
  andaman: { overview:'Andaman is a sea-and-islands escape built around clear water, relaxed beach time and marine experiences. Port Blair, Havelock and Neil Island combine well when ferry timings are planned carefully from the start.', planningNotes:'Ferries and water activities are weather-sensitive, so avoid a tightly packed itinerary. Carry sun protection, quick-dry clothing and valid government photo ID for island transfers.', idealDuration:'5 to 7 days', gettingThere:'Port Blair is the main arrival point, followed by scheduled inter-island ferries.', localInsight:'Keep one unplanned sunset evening on Havelock or Neil Island—those slower hours are often the highlight of the trip.' },
  rajasthan: { overview:'Rajasthan pairs grand forts and palaces with desert landscapes, craft traditions and some of India’s most characterful cities. Jaipur, Jodhpur, Udaipur and Jaisalmer can be combined into a cultural route with a strong sense of place.', planningNotes:'Days can be warm for much of the year, so plan outdoor sightseeing for mornings and late afternoons. Comfortable walking shoes are useful for forts, bazaars and old-city lanes.', idealDuration:'5 to 8 days', gettingThere:'Jaipur, Jodhpur and Udaipur have convenient air and rail connections; private road transfers link the classic circuit.', localInsight:'Add a local craft, food or heritage walk to balance landmark sightseeing with the everyday culture of each city.' },
  vietnam: { overview:'Vietnam rewards travellers with a contrast of old quarters, limestone scenery, lantern-lit towns, coffee culture and a remarkable food trail. Hanoi, Ha Long Bay, Da Nang and Hoi An create a natural first-time circuit.', planningNotes:'Build in breathing room between domestic transfers, keep an international card or local currency ready for small purchases, and check entry and visa requirements before booking flights.', idealDuration:'6 to 9 days', gettingThere:'Hanoi and Ho Chi Minh City are the main international gateways, with domestic flights and road transfers connecting regional stops.', localInsight:'A street-food walk and an unhurried evening in Hoi An add more depth than rushing between photo stops.' },
  'sri-lanka': { overview:'Sri Lanka fits tea-country rail views, wildlife, colonial-era towns and tropical beaches into one compact island journey. The classic route connects Colombo or Negombo, Kandy, Ella, the southern coast and a national park.', planningNotes:'Use a paced route because mountain roads and train journeys take time. Check visa requirements before departure and carry a light rain layer alongside beachwear.', idealDuration:'6 to 9 days', gettingThere:'Colombo is the primary gateway, followed by private transfers or scenic rail segments across the island.', localInsight:'The Kandy–Ella train is best experienced as part of the journey, not as a rushed transfer between two packed sightseeing days.' },
  bhutan: { overview:'Bhutan is a thoughtful Himalayan journey of dzongs, prayer flags, forest trails and quietly beautiful valleys. Thimphu, Punakha and Paro are a strong first itinerary for travellers who value culture and a slower pace.', planningNotes:'Travel formalities, entry permits and daily travel rules should be checked well before booking. Respectful clothing for monasteries and comfortable footwear for uneven paths are essential.', idealDuration:'5 to 7 days', gettingThere:'Paro is the main air gateway; road entry is also possible for eligible travellers through designated border points.', localInsight:'Leave time for a monastery visit or a short valley walk—Bhutan is best absorbed slowly, not ticked off quickly.' },
  dubai: { overview:'Dubai blends city-scale architecture, desert landscapes, beach clubs, malls and international dining in a compact, easy-to-navigate holiday. It works equally well as a short city break or as a stop combined with nearby Abu Dhabi.', planningNotes:'Reserve major experiences ahead during busy travel periods, dress for warm weather, and keep your passport and visa arrangements in order before departure.', idealDuration:'4 to 6 days', gettingThere:'Dubai International Airport is the main gateway, with city transfers by metro, taxi and private vehicle.', localInsight:'Balance the skyline with a desert sunset or creek-side evening for a more rounded experience of the city.' },
  maldives: { overview:'The Maldives is ideal for travellers who want a calm island stay centred on clear lagoons, house reefs and time to switch off. Choosing the right island or resort matters more than trying to move around frequently.', planningNotes:'Review transfer type and timings before booking, pack reef-safe sun protection, and confirm what meals, water activities and transfers are included in your stay.', idealDuration:'4 to 6 nights', gettingThere:'International arrivals land in Malé, followed by speedboat or seaplane transfers arranged by the property.', localInsight:'A few planned water activities plus generous free time is usually more rewarding than an over-scheduled island itinerary.' },
  singapore: { overview:'Singapore is a polished, high-energy city break with gardens, waterfront walks, neighbourhood food centres and family-friendly attractions. It is easy to navigate and pairs naturally with Sentosa, Marina Bay and cultural districts.', planningNotes:'Keep lightweight rain protection for quick tropical showers, use contactless payment where possible and reserve sought-after attractions in advance during peak dates.', idealDuration:'4 to 5 days', gettingThere:'Changi Airport connects directly to the city by taxi, rail and private transfer.', localInsight:'Hawker centres and neighbourhood walks around Kampong Glam, Chinatown or Little India reveal a more local side beyond the headline attractions.' },
  rishikesh: { overview:'Rishikesh is a quick reset built around the Ganga, yoga, cafés and Himalayan foothills. It suits first-time solo travellers, friend groups and anyone looking to pair easy adventure with a slower riverside rhythm.', planningNotes:'River activities depend on seasonal water conditions. Pack modest, comfortable clothing for ashram or temple visits and avoid overcommitting the short itinerary.', idealDuration:'2 to 4 days', gettingThere:'Rishikesh is reached easily from Delhi via Haridwar or Dehradun by rail and road.', localInsight:'Sunrise by the river and an evening aarti are often more memorable than trying to fit every adventure activity into one weekend.' },
  jibhi: { overview:'Jibhi is a gentle Himachal escape of cedar forests, wooden homes, waterfalls and quiet village lanes. It is best for travellers who prefer slow mornings, scenic drives and low-key mountain time over crowded resort towns.', planningNotes:'Connectivity can be patchy in nearby villages, so download maps and carry cash for small local purchases. Warm layers are useful throughout much of the year.', idealDuration:'3 to 4 days', gettingThere:'Most travellers arrive by road from Delhi or Chandigarh via Aut, Banjar and the Tirthan Valley region.', localInsight:'Keep your itinerary intentionally light—Jibhi’s appeal is the unhurried atmosphere, forest walks and homestay-style hospitality.' },
  chopta: { overview:'Chopta is a high-altitude meadow region known for big Himalayan views, simple stays and the Chandrashila–Tungnath trail. It is a rewarding short mountain escape for travellers comfortable with basic conditions.', planningNotes:'Weather changes fast and temperatures drop sharply after sunset. Carry sturdy shoes, warm layers, a reusable water bottle and allow buffer time for road conditions.', idealDuration:'3 to 5 days', gettingThere:'Road journeys typically route through Rishikesh, Rudraprayag and Ukhimath before reaching Chopta.', localInsight:'Start the Tungnath or Chandrashila walk early for clearer views and a less crowded trail experience.' },
  coorg: { overview:'Coorg offers coffee estates, misty roads, forest viewpoints and a comfortable slower pace in Karnataka’s Western Ghats. It works well for couples, families and Bangalore-based weekend travellers.', planningNotes:'Monsoon brings lush landscapes and frequent rain, while drier months are better for long outdoor days. Pack comfortable shoes for estate walks and viewpoint trails.', idealDuration:'3 to 4 days', gettingThere:'The journey is usually by road from Bengaluru or Mysuru, with the nearest major airports in those cities.', localInsight:'An estate stay, local Kodava meal and early-morning drive through the hills give Coorg more character than a checklist of viewpoints.' },
};
